<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iron Crown of Lombardy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Corona Ferrea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iron Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> is a medieval crown of gold and jewels, kept in the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a>, whose inner band was said to be made from a nail of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>. It was used to crown kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, including several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperors</a> in their Italian kingship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> crowned himself king of Italy with it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a> in 1805, and it remains a symbol of Italian kingship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iron-crown-of-lombardy.jpg" alt="Iron Crown of Lombardy" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Steakley · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iron_Crown.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to tradition, St. Helena, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>, had the crown forged for her son around a beaten nail from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>, which she had found. Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a> is said to have given it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a>, princess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, as a diplomatic gift, although he mentions no such donation among his recorded gifts, and Theodelinda gave it to the church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a> in 628.</p><p>Scholars have long debated its origin, since no contemporary sources record its making. Radiocarbon analysis in the late 20th century identified at least two phases: the oldest components, including organic materials used to set the gemstones, date to between the 5th and 6th centuries, and others to between the late 7th and 10th centuries. The gold plates are consistent with late Roman and Byzantine craftsmanship, and some scholars think the crown was once part of a ceremonial helmet or diadem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The crown has an outer circlet of six hinged segments of beaten gold, partly enamelled and set with 22 gemstones in relief, in the form of crosses and flowers. Inside is a band about one centimetre wide that is said to have been beaten from a nail of the crucifixion.</p><p>In 1993 the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> tested it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-ray</a> fluorescence and radiocarbon dating. The band was found to be pure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, with no iron. A note in the Roman Ceremonial of 1159 suggests the name came from a lamina of iron once laid over the crown, probably as an arch, rather than inside it. Beeswax under three enamel plates dated to around 500 and that under the others to around 800, consistent with a more ancient crown decorated under Theoderic and extensively restored under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kings did not use it, being acclaimed by the people in arms with the spear as symbol of royal power. Critical scholarship identifies only a limited number of the coronations that 17th-century writers attributed to it, the first confirmed being that of Otto III in 996 at Pavia, followed by Conrad II in 1026. Later confirmed uses include Charles IV in 1355, Sigismund in 1433 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1530 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, the last coronation performed by a pope.</p><p>On 26 May 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> crowned himself King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a> with it, saying, God has given it to me, woe to him who touches it. After being taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, it was returned to Italy in 1866 and restored to Monza Cathedral. It is kept in the Chapel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a> there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iron Crown” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 951 Otto I of Germany took the title, and from his imperial coronation in 962 the crown was tied to the German emperors, often crowned with the <b>Iron Crown of Lombardy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-italy-medieval">Kingdom of Italy (medieval)</a></li><li><blockquote>On 26 May 1805 Napoleon crowned himself king of Italy in Milan Cathedral with the <b>Iron Crown of Lombardy</b>, and appointed his stepson Eugène de Beauharnais as viceroy.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-italy-napoleonic">Kingdom of Italy (Napoleonic)</a></li></ul></section>
